# Rendered by `km install-units`: @TOKENS@ come from kingdom.toml + git.
#
# Fired by OnFailure= on kingdom-dispatch.service. The dispatcher is a oneshot
# on a two-minute timer, so a failing tick leaves nothing behind but a journal
# line nobody reads: on 2026-08-29 it exited 1 with no output on either stream
# for forty minutes while `km status` cheerfully printed a healthy board, and
# the outage was found by hand (bd gqlc-87al).
#
# This wakes Սեդրակ, and it is deliberately `km wake` rather than `km mail`.
# Mail wakes a seat only when the DISPATCHER notices it — so a letter about a
# dead dispatcher is delivered by the thing that is dead, and would sit unread
# for exactly as long as the fault it reports. `km wake` writes the seat's wake
# file, which the parked km-seat reads on its own, with no dispatcher involved.
#
# `km wake` is still tried FIRST, and the append below is a fallback, because
# km wake carries two things a bare append cannot: it respawns a missing runner
# (nothing parked on the queue means nobody reads it), and it nudges a seat that
# is already awake. Those matter in the failure class actually observed —
# gqlc-87al, a logic abort inside a km that runs fine — so they are not spent to
# buy the class below.
#
# But km wake rides @REPO@/kingdom/bin/km, which is the binary and checkout
# whose failure this unit announces. Measured out-of-tree against a scratch
# systemd --user (bd gqlc-leq7): with an unparseable km, and with km absent
# altogether, the dispatch tick failed, OnFailure fired, and the alarm failed by
# the same cause with nothing reaching Սեդրակ. The wake FILE is the half of
# km wake that needs no km — one deduplicated line appended to a path the parked
# km-seat reads on its own — so on any non-zero exit this appends it directly.
# That covers a km that cannot start, an unreadable kingdom.toml, and a checkout
# broken mid-operation. It does NOT cover a full or read-only filesystem, where
# the append fails too and the alarm goes quiet again; nothing one level up here
# would survive that either.
[Unit]
Description=Kingdom dispatch alarm — wake Սեդրակ when a dispatch tick fails

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=@REPO@
# The reason is passed as "$1" rather than written into the script, so that its
# own apostrophes stay inside a systemd double-quoted token and never reach the
# shell as quotes. `echo`, not `printf '%s\n'`: systemd unescapes backslashes in
# this value, so a format string here is one escaping level away from being read
# as a literal newline.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'd=@STATE@/seats/sedrak; @REPO@/kingdom/bin/km wake sedrak --reason "$1" && exit 0; r="$1 ALSO: km wake exited non-zero, so this line was appended by kingdom-dispatch-alarm.service itself — km may be the fault, not just its caller."; mkdir -p "$d" || exit 1; grep -Fxq -- "$r" "$d/wake" 2>/dev/null || echo "$r" >>"$d/wake"' sh "kingdom-dispatch.service FAILED — the town is not routing. Read 'journalctl --user -u kingdom-dispatch.service -n 50' before anything else; a failing tick is a town-wide outage, not one seat's problem."
